BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T190640Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:berggruen-prize-essay-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T190640Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: Berggruen Prize Essay Competition 2026: A New Axial Age?
DESCRIPTION:Annual essay competition from the Berggruen Institute\, a compa
 nion to the Berggruen Prize for Philosophy & Culture\, seeking new thinkin
 g and cross-cultural perspectives on fundamental philosophical questions o
 f significance for contemporary life and the future. It looks for essays i
 n the tradition of Rousseau\, Montaigne\, and Emerson (models cited includ
 e Frankfurt\, Nagel\, Sontag\, Fukuyama\, and recent Noema pieces)\, not p
 eer-reviewed academic work: novel ideas\, clearly argued in compelling way
 s for intellectually serious readers. THEME for 2026: 'A New Axial Age?' R
 eferencing Karl Jaspers's Axial Age (c. 800-200 BCE)\, the prompt asks whe
 ther civilisation is again at a 'hinge of history' undergoing a pivotal tr
 ansformation of consciousness driven by rapid technological change and its
  social\, economic\, and political consequences. Suggested (non-exhaustive
 ) angles: the foundational ideas and institutions underpinning present and
  future societies\; the philosophical implications of the current era\; th
 e future of human evolution and life amid the ecological crisis\; shared c
 onceptual foundations for a common future\; global transformation driven b
 y technology and the rise of AI\; the end of the global order and its rami
 fications\; and the future of life amid biotechnology and synthetic life. 
 Entrants may choose their own topic\; for 2026 creative fiction is accepte
 d. ELIGIBILITY: open to the public\; not for academic papers and not gover
 ned by academic disciplinary standards or affiliations. Length up to 10\,0
 00 words in English or 15\,000 characters in Chinese (excluding diagrams\,
  tables\, endnotes\, bibliography\, or authorship declaration). One submis
 sion per year per entrant\, in either English or Chinese\; co-authorship a
 llowed. Submissions must be original and previously unpublished. AI USAGE:
  generative AI must not be used to create content or substitute original t
 hought\, and entrants must engage primary sources rather than AI summaries
 \; AI is permitted only for proofreading/grammar\, as quoted supporting ma
 terial when AI itself is the subject of the research\, or for translating 
 an essay into English or Chinese\, and all AI use (including AI-assisted t
 ranslation) must be disclosed on the front page or the entry is disqualifi
 ed. JUDGING: originality\, clarity\, and style. Essays are due by 23:59 PT
  on 17 August 2026\; PDF only. Chinese-language submissions use a separate
  page. Inquiries to essay@berggruen.org.\n\nOrganisation: Berggruen Instit
 ute\nLocation: Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and C
 hinese submissions)\nAward: USD 50\,000 for the winning English essay and 
 USD 50\,000 for the winning Chinese essay. If there is more than one winne
 r in a language category\, the prize is split evenly. Winning essays are p
 ublished through BI Press or Noema. No entry fee.\nDuration: One-off compe
 tition\; submissions open 17 March 2026 and close 17 August 2026\n\nMore: 
 https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and Chinese 
 submissions)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:amr-paper-of-the-future-prize-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T190640Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: AMR: 2026 Paper of the Future Prize
DESCRIPTION:USD 10\,000 prize from the Association for Mathematical Researc
 h for serious experimentation in how mathematicians communicate with one a
 nother\, beyond the static PDF. Submissions should demonstrate communicati
 ve capabilities fundamentally unavailable in a linear paper: interactive e
 xploration of parameter spaces in differential equations\, dynamic visuali
 sation of group actions or geometric structures\, multi-perspective repres
 entations of algebraic or number-theoretic objects\, nonlinear navigation 
 of proof architectures or dependency graphs\, embedded computation as part
  of exposition\, or similar. ELIGIBILITY: no nationality\, institutional o
 r career restriction stated\; the focus is mathematical depth and communic
 ative innovation. This is NOT for popularisation\, production polish or sh
 ort-form video content - it is about novel research-to-research communicat
 ion. EVALUATION CRITERIA: mathematical depth and rigor\; conceptual insigh
 t enabled by the medium\; communicative innovation beyond static expositio
 n\; scalability and reproducibility\; transformative potential for researc
 h communication. PROCESS: initial submissions are a short public YouTube c
 oncept demonstration (with the hashtag #AMRPotF and a concise written expl
 anation of the mathematical substance and communicative innovation in the 
 video description)\, with the YouTube link emailed to PotFPrize@amathr.org
 . Finalists are asked to provide a fully accessible prototype suitable for
  hosting or linking within AMR Reviews\; the winning submission will be pu
 blished in AMR Reviews as an interactive exposition. Selection committee: 
 Mohammed Abouzaid\, Benson Farb\, Alex Kontorovich\, Akshay Venkatesh\, Ma
 ryna Viazovska.\n\nOrganisation: Association for Mathematical Research (AM
 R)\nLocation: Worldwide (digital submission)\nAward: USD 10\,000 prize to 
 the winner\; runner-up recognitions at committee discretion. The committee
  reserves the right to withhold the prize if no submissions meet the stand
 ards.\nDuration: Project-based\n\nMore: https://amathr.org/prizes/paper-of
 -the-future-prize-prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://amathr.org/prizes/paper-of-the-future-prize-prize/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide (digital submission)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:we-are-human-festival-call-for-films-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T190640Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: We Are Human Festival 2026: Call for Films (Paris)
DESCRIPTION:Call for short films (1-10 minutes) involving human/AI hybrid c
 reation\, from the We Are Human Foundation in Paris. Three cash awards: Gr
 and Prix EUR 5\,000\, Best Screenplay EUR 3\,000\, and an Ethics Award EUR
  2\,000. ELIGIBILITY: international\, applicants 18+\; films must have bee
 n completed after 1 June 2025 and involve human/AI hybrid creation\; a man
 datory Ethics Notebook accompanies each submission. No submission fee. Fes
 tival screening at Forum des images\, Paris\, on 24 November 2026. Apply v
 ia FilmFreeway\; deadline 30 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: We Are Human
  Foundation\nLocation: Paris\, France (Forum des images screening 24 Novem
 ber 2026)\nAward: Grand Prix EUR 5\,000\; Best Screenplay EUR 3\,000\; Eth
 ics Award EUR 2\,000. No submission fee.\nDuration: Annual short-film fest
 ival (screening 24 November 2026)\n\nMore: https://wearehuman.foundation/e
 n/call-for-films/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://wearehuman.foundation/en/call-for-films/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Forum des images screening 24 November 2026)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:next-art-ai-award-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T190640Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI ART AWARD: NEXT ART AI 2026
DESCRIPTION:NEXT ART AI is an open AI art competition run by the HOBAN Cult
 ural Foundation\, welcoming participants of all backgrounds regardless of 
 career\, nationality or age\, and framed as a way to reflect on the future
  of AI art through new modes of expression and artistic experimentation. T
 WO CATEGORIES: AI IMAGE ART\, for works in a single-image format at 150dpi
  or higher\, submitted as JPG\, JPEG\, PNG or BMP up to 10MB\; and AI VIDE
 O ART\, open to a wide range of video formats including animation\, motion
  graphics and live-action footage enhanced or transformed using AI\, at 10
 80p or higher\, under one minute\, 30 or 60fps recommended\, stereo 48kHz 
 audio recommended\, as mp4 (h.264) or mov. Video entries are submitted as 
 a URL via YouTube\, Vimeo\, Dropbox or OneDrive\, or an alternative platfo
 rm if those are restricted in your region\; links that are inaccessible du
 e to privacy settings may be excluded from judging. WHO CAN ENTER: individ
 uals or teams of up to three including the representative\, though you may
  not submit both as an individual and as part of a team. Team representati
 ves create the account in their own name and all members submit a consent 
 form for personal data. Applicants under 19 need a guardian's signed conse
 nt. One work may be submitted per category. RULES ON AI AND ASSETS: work m
 ust be original and created by the applicant or team\, and must be image o
 r video art created using generative AI platforms. Works that have previou
 sly won an award in another competition are not eligible. Free or paid ext
 ernal assets\, game engines\, AI frameworks\, open-source tools and editin
 g software may be used freely\, but applicants must hold licences permitti
 ng commercial use for every element\, and work infringing third-party righ
 ts is excluded. Even where external assets and editing software are used\,
  the creative process must involve AI\, whether AI-assisted direction\, tr
 ansformation\, generation or other AI-based processes\, and the choice of 
 AI tools\, the scope and method of their use\, and the prompts themselves 
 are all weighed in evaluating the creative use of AI. TIMELINE: applicatio
 ns open 1 September 2026 and close 30 September 2026 at 11:59pm KST\, prel
 iminary round early October\, final round late October\, announcement and 
 ceremony early November\, exhibition late November 2026. Organised and hos
 ted by the HOBAN Cultural Foundation\, sponsored by HOBAN Group\, with Seo
 ul Shinmun and EBN as media partners. Details and schedule are subject to 
 change at the organiser's discretion.\n\nOrganisation: HOBAN Cultural Foun
 dation\nLocation: Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibit
 ion in South Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
 \nAward: Total prize pool of KRW 21\,000\,000 across 20 winners\, paid aft
 er deduction of applicable taxes and public charges. Grand Prize KRW 5\,00
 0\,000 (selected regardless of category)\; in each of the two categories\,
  First Prize KRW 2\,000\,000\, three Second Prizes of KRW 1\,000\,000 each
 \, and five Honorable Finalist awards of KRW 500\,000 each\; plus a HOBAN 
 Group President's Award of KRW 1\,000\,000. Winners also receive plaques a
 nd certificates\, online and offline exhibition opportunities\, and a feat
 ure on the official online platform. No entry fee is mentioned.\nDuration:
  One-off competition\; exhibition late November 2026\n\nMore: https://next
 -art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://next-art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibition in Sou
 th Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
